-## Disaster Recovery and Restoration
+## Step-by-Step Operator Runbooks
+
+### Runbook 1: Creating an Encrypted Backup Snapshot
 
-Restoring a cluster from an encrypted backup archive is completely automated.
+1. Navigate to `/admin/standalone` and open the **Backups** tab.
+2. Under **Manual Encrypted Backup**, enter an encryption passphrase meeting the 16-character minimum requirement.
+3. Click **Download Backup (.kbbak)**. The server streams the encrypted `KBKP` archive directly to your browser.
+4. Alternatively, click **Create Server Snapshot Now** to invoke the background daemon. The new snapshot appears in the Server Snapshots table with its exact byte size and timestamp.
 
-```bash
-# Step 1: Validate archive integrity without restoring
-kanban-ctl backup verify --input /var/backups/kanban-snapshot.kbbak1
+### Runbook 2: Restoring from an Encrypted Backup Archive
 
-# Step 2: Execute full disaster recovery restoration
-kanban-ctl backup restore \
-  --input /var/backups/kanban-snapshot.kbbak1 \
-  --decrypt \
-  --confirm-overwrite
+Restoring a database snapshot completely replaces existing database state with the archive contents. Follow this two-stage procedure:
+
+1. In `/admin/standalone`, scroll to **Restore Database Snapshot**.
+2. Select an existing server snapshot from the dropdown, or upload a local `.kbbak` archive file.
+3. Enter the exact passphrase used when creating the backup.
+4. Click **Verify Archive Integrity**. The backend validates the GCM authentication tag and verifies internal component checksums without touching the database.
+5. In the confirmation input box, type the uppercase confirmation phrase `RESTORE`.
+6. Click **Execute Destructive Restore**. The backend enters single-user maintenance mode, terminates existing sessions, restores all tables and sequences, re-establishes RLS policies, and reconnects the application.
+
+```mermaid
+sequenceDiagram
+    participant Admin as Administrator UI
+    participant API as FastAPI Backend (/api/v1/admin/standalone/*)
+    participant Ops as Ops Daemon (/var/lib/kanban/.kb_ops.sock)
+    participant DB as PostgreSQL 16 (kanban)
+
+    Admin->>API: POST /restore (archive, passphrase, confirm_overwrite="RESTORE")
+    API->>API: Validate confirm_overwrite == "RESTORE" (422 if mismatched)
+    API->>API: Derive AES-256 key via PBKDF2 (600,000 rounds)
+    API->>API: Verify AES-256-GCM auth tag (422 if tampered/wrong passphrase)
+    API->>Ops: IPC RESTORE_SNAPSHOT(temp_archive_path)
+    Ops->>DB: Terminate connections & drop/re-create tables
+    Ops->>DB: Ingest database.sql.gz & restore sequences
+    Ops->>DB: Execute audit-rls (assert FORCE RLS on all tables)
+    Ops-->>API: Restore completed (100 tables restored)
+    API-->>Admin: HTTP 200 OK (Restore Successful)
 ```
 
-### Post-Restore Automated Verification
-Following database restoration:
-1. All database sequences are automatically synchronized.
-2. The **Invalid Object Link Auditor** executes immediately to verify referential integrity.
-3. RLS policies are audited to confirm that `kanban_app` lacks bypass permissions.
-4. Active WebSocket sessions are broadcast a reconnect signal.
+### Runbook 3: Promoting Standalone to Production Multi-Container Compose
+
+1. In `/admin/standalone`, switch to the **Docker Compose Export** tab.
+2. Click **Download Production Compose Bundle**. The browser downloads a `.zip` or `.tar.gz` archive containing `docker-compose.yml`, `Caddyfile`, and `.env.example`.
+3. Extract the bundle on your production host:
+   ```bash
+   mkdir -p /opt/kanban-prod && cd /opt/kanban-prod
+   unzip ~/Downloads/kanban-compose-export.zip
+   ```
+4. Copy your standalone PostgreSQL data volume:
+   ```bash
+   docker run --rm -v kanban_data:/from:ro -v kanban_prod_pgdata:/to alpine cp -a /from/. /to/
+   ```
+5. Review `.env.example`, populate production domain names and TLS certificates, and rename to `.env`.
+6. Start the production stack:
+   ```bash
+   docker compose up -d
+   ```
+7. Verify container status and network compartmentalization:
+   ```bash
+   docker compose ps
+   docker compose exec app kanban-ctl health --full
+   ```
+
